.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt KUBE-STATUS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kube-status
.Nd one report on the Kubernetes cluster this machine runs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints, in order: the cluster's addresses; its nodes; Cilium's status;
the pods in
.Ql kube-system ;
the namespaces; node resource use, when metrics-server is installed; the
ZFS datasets and snapshots whose names mention etcd, containerd, the
kubelet or kube, with the first ten snapshots and a count; and whether
Hubble's relay is installed, with the command that opens its interface.
A section whose command fails says so, and the report goes on.
.Pp
It reads the cluster's admin configuration, so it runs itself under
.Xr sudo 8 .
It changes nothing.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print what it shows and exit 0, without root.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when it reported; 1, on standard error, when there is no
.Xr kubectl 1
or it cannot reach a cluster \(em the answer on a worker or on a machine
with no cluster.
.Sh EXAMPLES
On a kldload desktop with no cluster, on 2026-09-30, with this release's
.Nm
first on the path:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ kube-status
[kube-status] No cluster available. Is this a control plane node?
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Pp
On a control plane; not run for this page, as no cluster was up:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kube-status
# prints the sections above, each under its own heading
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kubectl 1 ,
.Xr kube-cluster 8 ,
.Xr kube-network 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-30, writing this page: on a machine with more than ten
matching snapshots the snapshot section printed ten and then
.Dq None ,
and any
.Xr kubectl 1
call that failed ended the report part-way without saying where.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
The snapshot section matches by name, so a machine holding another
host's backups lists those too.
